From: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8350-b2q: bring up the SN100U at 0x2b

Stock describes the NFC controller twice on this bus: a QTI
"qcom,sn-nci" node at 0x28 (1 MHz, pinctrl nfc_*_active) and Samsung's
"pn547" node at 0x2b (400 kHz, no pinctrl). The HAL opens /dev/pn547,
so the second one is what stock runs, and the SN100U answers at 0x2b.

Follow it: move the node to 0x2b, run the bus at 400 kHz and enable
it. The pins stay at the TLMM reset state, as on stock, where nothing
applies the QTI node's states: pulled down. The pull-up copied from
nfc_int_active held the level-high interrupt line asserted while the
controller was off, which flooded the CPU with interrupts (40 million
in four minutes) and left nxp-nci in its hard-fault state, so the reply
to the first CORE_RESET was never read. It also made the clock-request
line read high with the controller off.

With the sm8350 eGPIO fix, which lets GPIO 201 (firmware download)
drive low, the controller powers up through nxp-nci, completes NCI
init and detects a tag (a YubiKey 5C NFC) while polling.

Signed-off-by: Noa Himesaka <himesaka@noa.codes>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350-samsung-b2q.dts |   41 +++++++++++++----------
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+), 17 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350-samsung-b2q.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350-samsung-b2q.dts
index dab7426..14313f7 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350-samsung-b2q.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8350-samsung-b2q.dts
@@ -1405,24 +1405,32 @@
 		bias-disable;
 	};
 
-	/* Stock nfc_int_active: the SN100U's interrupt on GPIO 87 */
+	/*
+	 * The NFC pins below keep the configuration stock really runs with.
+	 * Stock's pn547 node has no pinctrl, and the nfc_*_active states belong
+	 * to the unused QTI node, which does not apply them by itself. So the
+	 * pins stay at the TLMM reset state, pulled down. A pull-up on the
+	 * level-high interrupt line keeps it asserted while the controller is
+	 * off, which floods the CPU with interrupts.
+	 */
+	/* The SN100U's interrupt on GPIO 87 */
 	nfc_int_state: nfc-int-state {
 		pins = "gpio87";
 		function = "gpio";
 		drive-strength = <2>;
-		bias-pull-up;
+		bias-pull-down;
 		input-enable;
 	};
 
-	/* Stock nfc_enable_active: VEN on GPIO 62 */
+	/* VEN on GPIO 62 */
 	nfc_enable_state: nfc-enable-state {
 		pins = "gpio62";
 		function = "gpio";
 		drive-strength = <2>;
-		bias-pull-up;
+		bias-pull-down;
 	};
 
-	/* Stock nfc_fwdl_active: firmware download on GPIO 201 (NFC_FIRM) */
+	/* Firmware download on GPIO 201 (NFC_FIRM), an eGPIO */
 	nfc_fwdl_state: nfc-fwdl-state {
 		pins = "gpio201";
 		function = "gpio";
@@ -1430,12 +1438,12 @@
 		bias-disable;
 	};
 
-	/* Stock nfc_clk_req_active: the controller's clock request on GPIO 63 */
+	/* The controller's clock request on GPIO 63 */
 	nfc_clkreq_state: nfc-clkreq-state {
 		pins = "gpio63";
 		function = "gpio";
 		drive-strength = <2>;
-		bias-pull-up;
+		bias-pull-down;
 		input-enable;
 	};
 
@@ -1854,20 +1862,19 @@
 
 &i2c15 {
 	status = "okay";
-	/* Stock runs the SN100 bus at 1 MHz. */
-	clock-frequency = <1000000>;
+	/* Stock's pn547 node runs the bus at 400 kHz. */
+	clock-frequency = <400000>;
 
 	/*
-	 * The controller is an NXP SN100U (stock compatible "qcom,sn-nci",
-	 * HAL lib nfc_nci_nxpsn), not the PN547 the stock tree also carries
-	 * for other variants. It is NCI 2.0 and the mainline nxp-nci driver
-	 * speaks to it. VEN 62, IRQ 87, FW 201 (NFC_FIRM), CLKREQ 63.
+	 * The controller is an NXP SN100U (NCI 2.0, HAL lib nfc_nci_nxpsn).
+	 * Stock carries two nodes for it on this bus, QTI "qcom,sn-nci" at
+	 * 0x28 and Samsung "pn547" at 0x2b; the HAL opens /dev/pn547, and the
+	 * chip answers at 0x2b. VEN 62, IRQ 87, FW 201 (NFC_FIRM), CLKREQ 63.
+	 * GPIO 201 is an eGPIO: it only drives once APPS claims it.
 	 */
-	nfc: nfc@28 {
+	nfc: nfc@2b {
 		compatible = "nxp,pn553", "nxp,nxp-nci-i2c";
-		reg = <0x28>;
-		/* Disabled until the controller's clock/power is understood. */
-		status = "disabled";
+		reg = <0x2b>;
 
 		interrupts-extended = <&tlmm 87 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
 		enable-gpios = <&tlmm 62 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH>;
